F29a56b2f41deabc5b8f65805d9f9%2Fgettyimages-2298077242.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4153x2336+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbe%2F12%2F29a56b2f41deabc5b8f65805d9f9%2Fgettyimages-2298077242.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4153x2336+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbe%2F12%2F29a56b2f41deabc5b8f65805d9f9%2Fgettyimages-2298077242.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4153x2336+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fbe%2F12%2F29a56b2f41deabc5b8f65805d9f9%2Fgettyimages-2298077242.jpg" class="img" alt="A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket and Dragon spacecraft carrying NASA&amp;#039;s Crew-13 lifts off Thursday from Cape Canaveral, Fla. SpaceX&amp;#039;s Crew Dragon capsule &amp;quot;Grace&amp;quot; will take NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins and Luke Delaney, Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutryk, and Roscosmos cosmonaut Sergey Teteryatnikov to the International Space Station for about six months." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket and Dragon spacecraft carrying NASA's Crew-13 lifts off Thursday from Cape Canaveral, Fla. SpaceX's Crew Dragon capsule "Grace" will take NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins and Luke Delaney, Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutryk, and Roscosmos cosmonaut Sergey Teteryatnikov to the International Space Station for about six months.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Justin Sullivan/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;SpaceX launched four astronauts on &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/01/nx-s1-5987698/nasa-crew-13-spacex-international-space-station-iss" target="_blank"&gt;NASA's Crew-13 mission&lt;/a&gt; to the International Space Station for a six-month stay before returning to Earth.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Google &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/01/nx-s1-5983697/project-suncatcher-google-ai-data-center-space" target="_blank"&gt;launched a refrigerator-sized satellite&lt;/a&gt; equipped with AI chips as part of Project Suncatcher, its effort to put AI data centers in space.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The Pearly Kings were once a fixture of East London, raising money in their mother-of-pearl suits. Now, NPR &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/01/nx-s1-5928148/pearly-king-peckham-george-major-london-cockney" target="_blank"&gt;looks back at George Major&lt;/a&gt;, who died in August, and the tradition he helped carry on.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/1155154221/majd-al-waheidi"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Majd Al-Waheidi&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
