      <title>U.S. forces exit Iraq amid Iran war. And, SCOTUS revives Trump deportation policy</title>
      <author>Brittney Melton</author>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/g-s1-145604/up-first-newsletter-iraq-us-troops-scotus-deportations-trump-ai</link>
      <comments>https://text.npr.org/2026/09/30/g-s1-145604/up-first-newsletter-iraq-us-troops-scotus-deportations-trump-ai</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/g-s1-145604/up-first-newsletter-iraq-us-troops-scotus-deportations-trump-ai</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:13:17 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The last U.S. forces have left Iraq&lt;/strong&gt;, more than two decades after the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/nx-s1-5983426/u-s-withdraws-final-troops-from-iraq" target="_blank"&gt;U.S.-led invasion that overthrew Saddam Hussein&lt;/a&gt;. Iraq has declared today "Sovereignty Day." The withdrawal comes as attacks linked to the U.S.-Iran war raise new questions about Iraq's ability to defend itself without U.S. air defenses.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-145605-1" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 6000;         --source-height: 3375;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x3375+0+313/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F4c%2F57%2Fbf5fb6714747859e293114dddcc3%2Fgettyimages-2297194981.jpg" class="img" alt="A US Air Force C-130 Hercules military transport aircraft prepares for takeoff from Camp Victory on the outskirts of Baghdad International Airport on Sept. 29, 2026." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A US Air Force C-130 Hercules military transport aircraft prepares for takeoff from Camp Victory on the outskirts of Baghdad International Airport on Sept. 29, 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Ammar Karim/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The Kurdish region of Iraq has been hit hard&lt;/strong&gt; during the U.S. war with Iran, leaving its leaders concerned about their defenses, NPR's Jane Arraf tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. This is not the first U.S. withdrawal from Iraq: Troops also left in 2011, before returning three years later to help fight ISIS. Iran-backed militias are celebrating the withdrawal. Arraf says many Iraqis enjoying the holiday today want what they have &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5985698:x" target="_blank"&gt;long wanted for their families&lt;/a&gt;: a normal, peaceful life.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Listen to &lt;em&gt;Morning Edition&lt;/em&gt;'s conversation with retired Lt. Gen. H. R. McMaster&lt;/strong&gt;, former U.S. National Security adviser, about how the final withdrawal of U.S. troops from Iraq &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/nx-s1-5984953/the-u-s-is-pulling-its-last-troops-out-of-iraq-how-will-it-affect-the-middle-east" target="_blank"&gt;impacts the Middle East&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The U.S. Supreme Court on Tuesday allowed the Trump administration to resume&lt;/strong&gt;, for now, deporting migrants to countries not listed in their removal orders. The court will hear full arguments in December. The court didn't explain &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/g-s1-145266/supreme-court-trump-third-country-deportations" target="_blank"&gt;why it paused a lower-court ruling&lt;/a&gt; blocking the policy but it asked both sides whether lower courts had authority to intervene and whether the policy violates due process or protections against torture.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; Third-country removals allow the government to send immigrants elsewhere&lt;/strong&gt; when their home countries refuse to accept them, NPR's Linda Kenyon says. Immigration advocates warn the policy could &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5985698:x" target="_blank"&gt;cause irreparable damage&lt;/a&gt;. One immigrant challenging the policy says he fears being sent to a country where he would not receive treatment for his severe mental illness.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;Mahmoud Khalil, who became a prominent face of Columbia University's pro-Palestinian movement&lt;/strong&gt;, is &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/nx-s1-5984548/mahmoud-khalil-columbia-ice-palestine" target="_blank"&gt;also fighting the Trump administration's effort&lt;/a&gt; to deport him. He told&lt;em&gt; Fresh Air&lt;/em&gt; that 104 days in immigration detention strengthened his resolve to challenge the case.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;President Trump says that tech companies can largely self-regulate &lt;/strong&gt;when it comes to artificial intelligence. After meeting with top AI executives Tuesday, he signed an executive order referring to the technology as "superintelligence" for government agencies and launched an AI-powered website, America.gov.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag rte2-style-ul"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Trump said the Justice Department or FBI could step in&lt;/strong&gt; if companies break the law — but NPR's Emily Feng notes there are no new AI laws behind the meeting. Executives from six major AI companies signed a one-page document pledging to monitor their models and prevent harmful uses such as hacking. Asked whether the agreement was enforceable, Trump called it &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5985698:x" target="_blank"&gt;"morally binding"&lt;/a&gt; and said the companies would be "policing each other."&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Picture show&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-143848" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 6240;         --source-height: 3510;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6240x3510+0+325/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F42%2F76%2F8c3ef0e44c1cbce3b20fea03b1b2%2Flesotho-shepherds-73.jpg" class="img" alt="Shepherds attend a part time night school near Mokhotlong, Lesotho. For many, these classes, which occur roughly twice a month, are the only form of education they can access." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Shepherds attend a part time  night school near Mokhotlong, Lesotho. For many, these classes, which occur roughly twice a month, are the only form of education they can access.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Tommy Trenchard for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;In Lesotho, a small mountainous country surrounded by South Africa, young shepherds can spend months tending livestock in remote areas, often missing school. The Good Shepherd School offers flexible evening classes in reading, writing and counting. Check out these photos to see &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/27/g-s1-143582/night-school-shepherds-lesotho" target="_blank"&gt;how these shepherds are balancing work and education&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Behind the story&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;by &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/495064735/meg-anderson" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Meg Anderson&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, National Desk correspondent&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-144292" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4200;         --source-height: 2363;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4200x2363+0+219/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F5c%2F30%2Fbbec3f34414a827e0820aaf3b6a0%2Fheart-npr-cw-3602.jpg" class="img" alt="Durham, N.C. - September 14th, 2026: Rob Crisp, a peer support specialist with the HEART program, responds to a call for a wellness check at a parking lot." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Durham, N.C. - September 14th, 2026: Rob Crisp, a peer support specialist with the HEART program, responds to a call for a wellness check at a parking lot.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Cornell Watson for NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Since the 2020 police killing of George Floyd, programs that send unarmed responders to some 911 calls instead of police have quietly grown — from just six in 2020 to nearly 100 today.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;I wanted to understand what it takes to do that work well, so I spent a few days with the community response program in Durham, N.C.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;From the back of their minivan, I watched responders handle 911 calls about things like trespassing and panhandling. They doled out bottled water, snacks and fresh socks, and defused some tough situations: An agitated man refusing to leave a parking lot, a paranoid man asking to go to the hospital, a woman needing a safer place to live.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;But they often had few long-term solutions to offer.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Researchers told me this tension is common. The goal after 2020 was to avoid more police killings, not solve problems like homelessness. Now, Durham and dozens of other cities are wrestling with this bigger question: Is success resolving the crisis? Or is it to offer a path to stability, so that eventually fewer 911 calls are made in the first place?&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Read &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/22/nx-s1-5973302/unarmed-911-responders-police-reform" target="_blank"&gt;my full reporting here&lt;/a&gt;. And if you have a story or tip about policing and public safety in your city or elsewhere, you can reach me on the encrypted messaging app Signal at &lt;a href="https://signal.me/#eu/zFProiRfXk34fsS9NeSlOXEKQ2uRkEWNO4TYKaTORZTG1ok89UxK3JSNWaRJVA33" target="_blank"&gt;mma.05&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="res1228482847" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2921;         --source-height: 1643;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=400&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 400w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=600&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 600w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=800&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 800w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=900&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 900w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1200&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 1200w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1600&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 1600w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1800&amp;c=85&amp;f=webp 1800w" data-template="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s={width}&amp;c={quality}&amp;f={format}" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=400&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 400w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=600&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 600w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=800&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 800w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=900&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 900w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1200&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 1200w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1600&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 1600w, https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1800&amp;c=85&amp;f=jpeg 1800w" data-template="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s={width}&amp;c={quality}&amp;f={format}" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s=1100&amp;c=50&amp;f=jpeg" data-template="https://media.npr.org/assets/img/2024/02/01/spc-lead-graphic-web-final-1-_wide-b16c6ce248b1a2760985497a063021dc3951bfde.jpg?s={width}&amp;c={quality}&amp;f={format}" class="img" alt="Student Podcast Challenge" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt;          &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;      &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag rte2-style-ol"&gt;&lt;li&gt;NPR staff listened to nearly 1,500 podcasts and picked 20 finalists for this year's Student Podcast Challenge. &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/nx-s1-5930430/student-podcast-challenge-2026-finalists" target="_blank"&gt;Hear the finalists&lt;/a&gt; and see the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/30/nx-s1-5984991/student-podcast-challenge-2026-honorable-mentions" target="_blank"&gt;honorable mentions&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Cathy Tie wants to show that her startup's gene-editing technology &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/nx-s1-5950439/embryo-gene-editing-babies-biotech" target="_blank"&gt;could repair disease-causing mutations&lt;/a&gt; in embryos and help prevent inherited diseases. Editing genes in human embryos is illegal in the U.S.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;A saxophonist, a forensic anthropologist and a historian are among this year's 20 MacArthur fellows — each receiving an $800,000 "genius grant." &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/nx-s1-5980447/macarthur-genius-grants-2026-fellows" target="_blank"&gt;Meet the winners&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/1155154221/majd-al-waheidi"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Majd Al-Waheidi&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
